# --- OS / editor noise ---
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# --- Python (test suite) ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- Claude Code local settings ---
# Personal, machine-local runtime state — never shared. The `**/` prefix is load-bearing: a pattern
# with a slash in the middle is anchored to THIS directory, so a bare `.claude/…` rule would cover the
# repo root and silently miss `src/.claude/…` — where these files actually appear when you run Chief
# of Staff from the source tree.
**/.claude/settings.local.json
**/.claude/scheduled_tasks.lock

# Subagents. Split by tree, unlike the rule above: root .claude/agents/ holds the development
# specialists — versioned, part of the build harness, reviewed like any other repo file.
# src/.claude/agents/ is a LEGACY guard: the product stopped generating per-principal member shims in
# 0.22.0 (brain/schemas/team-member.md -> "Why there is no subagent projection"), but an older install
# may still have one, so keep it uncommittable. build-dist.sh strips the directory regardless.
/src/.claude/agents/

# --- Memory ---
# src/memory/ is one principal's data and is never committed. It exists in the source tree because
# src/ is a complete, runnable install (that's how you try a change). The rule lives HERE, not in an
# in-folder .gitignore, so a principal can version their own memory/ in a SEPARATE git repo without a
# committed `*` rule fighting them. A tracked .keep holds the empty folder in the ZIP — named without
# the git prefix because it ships, and an install is a folder someone unzipped, not a checkout.
/src/memory/*
!/src/memory/.keep

# Built distributable ZIPs (release artifacts)
/dist/

# Machine-local tooling. Every tracked file here is published, so anything that should stay private —
# scripts wired to a personal checkout, notes, throwaway helpers — lives in here and never lands in a
# commit. Check #16 enforces the other half of that boundary.
/.local/

# Node. This repo has no dependencies and no build step that needs any: build-dist.sh is shell plus
# `zip`, and the structural suite is stdlib Python. The rule stays because the suite lints every
# TRACKED .md via `git ls-files`, so one committed node_modules/ floods it with third-party READMEs —
# that happened once (2,222 files) via `git add -A`. Anything needing npm belongs to the site repo.
node_modules/

# Subagent-driven-development scratch: the ledger, briefs and review packages it keeps, and the
# plans/specs it writes. Working notes for one machine's sessions — never committed.
/.superpowers/
/docs/superpowers/
